ecreasing sequences needed to partition a permutation is equal to the length of the longest increasing subsequence (Dilworth). But here we are partitioning the rows into decreasing subsequences based on w_i. However, each decreasing subsequence corresponds to a rectangle that covers those rows and columns up to the smallest w_i among them. But does that rectangle necessarily cover exactly those rows? It would also cover all columns from 1 up to that smallest w_i, which includes squares that belong to other rows not in the subsequence (if those rows have w_i >= that smallest w_i). But those squares would be covered by the rectangle as well, which may be okay because we want to cover all squares in L. However, the rectangle may also cover squares in rows not in the subsequence, which would be okay as long as those squares are also covered by some rectangle (maybe the same rectangle). But then the rectangle would also cover rows not in the subsequence, which means the rectangle's row set is not exactly the subsequence but includes additional rows. So the mapping is not one-to-one.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the solution's mapping is: Each decreasing subsequence corresponds to a rectangle that covers exactly the rows in that subsequence, but not any other rows, by using the fact that the rectangle's right side is at the smallest w_i among those rows, and left side is column 1. However, if there is a row between those rows (i.e., not in the subsequence) with w_i >= that smallest w_i, then the rectangle would also cover that row's squares in columns 1..c. That would be undesirable because those squares would be covered twice (if also covered by other rectangles). But maybe we can avoid that by ensuring that the decreasing subsequence picks rows that are consecutive? But decreasing subsequence does not guarantee consecutiveness.\n\nThus the mapping is not straightforward.\n\nLet's examine known literature: There is a known result about covering the complement of a permutation