olumn 1 is indeed a property of a \"horizontal strip\" covering the left region: Since L is defined as the set of cells left of the permutation matrix, each row i has cells from column 1 to p(i)-1. So any rectangle that lies entirely within L can be shifted leftwards until its left side is at column 1, as long as all rows covered have at least that many columns. However, if some row has width less than the rectangle's leftmost column, then we cannot shift leftwards. But the rectangle's leftmost column must be <= min_{i in rows} w_i + 1? Actually, if we have rows a..b, each row i has width w_i = p(i)-1. The rectangle's leftmost column L must satisfy L >= 1 and L <= w_i for each i (since rectangle must be within columns 1..w_i). So the rectangle's leftmost column must be <= min_i w_i. So we can shift leftwards to column 1 only if min_i w_i >= 1 (i.e., all rows have at least one cell). If some row has w_i = 0 (i.e., p(i) = 1), then that row has no left region. Then any rectangle covering that row cannot exist in L. So any rectangle covering rows that have w_i = 0 cannot be in L. So any rectangle in L must be formed by rows with w_i >= 1. For those rows, we can shift leftwards to column 1 because column 1 is within the left region for each such row. So indeed any rectangle in L can be assumed to start at column 1, because we can always shift leftwards until column 1 without leaving L (since column 1 is present in each row of the rectangle). However, if some row has w_i = 0, then that row cannot be part of the rectangle. So the rectangle's rows must be a subset of rows where w_i >= 1. So the rectangle's left side can be column 1. So the claim that any rectangle in L must have left side on column 1 is valid if we consider only rectangles covering rows where w_i >= 1. But is it possible to have a rectangle that starts at column >1 but still within L? Suppose we have rows i where w_i >= 5, and we choose a rectangle covering columns 2..4. Then we could shift leftwards to columns 1..3, which still lies within L for those rows, because column 1 is present. So we can always shift leftwards to column 1. So any rectangle can be transformed into one that starts at column 1, covering the same rows and possibly covering more squares (the leftmost column). But that would increase the area covered, possibly overlapping with other rectangles? However, we can adjust the tiling: If we shift leftwards, we may cause overlap with other rectangles or uncovered squares? But we can always modify the tiling to have rectangles anchored at column 1 without increasing the number of rectangles? Let's think: Suppose we have a tiling of L with rectangles that may start at various columns. We could transform each rectangle to start at column 1 by extending it leftwards to include column 1 for the rows it covers. This may cause overlapping with other rectangles that also cover column 1 for overlapping rows. But we could then merge overlapping rectangles? This is not trivial. So the claim that any rectangle must have left side on column 1 is not obviously true. However, perhaps we can argue that there exists an optimal tiling where each rectangle is anchored at column 1. Because if a rectangle does not start at column 1, we can slide it leftwards until it hits column 1 or until it hits a row where w_i = 0, but that row is not part of the rectangle. So we can slide it leftwards without affecting other rectangles because the region left of the rectangle is empty (i.e., not covered by any other rectangle) because any rectangle covering those rows must also be anchored at column 1 (maybe). But we need to be careful.\n\nNevertheless, the solution's argument may be considered valid if we accept that any rectangle can be assumed to start at column 1 without loss of generality. This is a known technique in such problems: For the left region, we can assume rectangles are anchored at the left border because any rectangle that is not anchored can be slid leftwards without intersecting uncovered squares or other rectangles, because the region left of it is empty (since it's part of L). However, we need to ensure that sliding does not cause overlap with other rectangles that also occupy those rows. But if we slide leftwards, we may overlap with other rectangles that also occupy those rows but perhaps are anchored at column 1. But we could merge them into a larger rectangle, reducing the number of rectangles. So an optimal tiling can be assumed to have rectangles anchored at column 1. This is plausible.\n\nThus the mapping may be valid.\n\nNow, the solution uses Dilworth's theorem to claim the minimal number of decreasing subsequences needed to partition the sequence w_i equals LIS(p). This is correct: In the poset where i j and w_i <= w_j, the width is LIS. Actually, Dilworth's theorem states that the size of a minimum chain decomposition equals the size of a maximum antichain. For a sequence, the poset is defined by i j and w_i <= w_j (i.e., increasing subsequences correspond to chains). Then the minimum number of antichains needed to partition the set equals the size of the longest chain (LIS). But the solution uses decreasing subsequences, which correspond to antichains? Let's recall: In a permutation, an increasing subsequence is a set of indices i1 i2 ... ik such that p(i1) p(i2) ... p(ik). That's a chain in the partial order where (i,p(i)) are points with the product order. A decreasing subsequence is an antichain? Actually, a decreasing subsequence is a set of indices i1 i2 ... ik such that p(i1) > p(i2) > ... > p(ik). This is a chain in the order where i1 i2 and p(i1) > p(i2) (i.e., opposite direction). But the partial order we consider for Dilworth's theorem is the product order: (i1, p(i1)) <= (i2, p(i2)) if i1 <= i2 and p(i1) <= p(i2). Then a chain corresponds to an increasing subsequence, and an antichain corresponds to a decreasing subsequence (since for a decreasing subsequence, you cannot have both coordinates increasing).