ecting uncovered squares or other rectangles, because the region left of it is empty (since it's part of L). However, we need to ensure that sliding does not cause overlap with other rectangles that also occupy those rows. But if we slide leftwards, we may overlap with other rectangles that also occupy those rows but perhaps are anchored at column 1. But we could merge them into a larger rectangle, reducing the number of rectangles. So an optimal tiling can be assumed to have rectangles anchored at column 1. This is plausible.\n\nThus the mapping may be valid.\n\nNow, the solution uses Dilworth's theorem to claim the minimal number of decreasing subsequences needed to partition the sequence w_i equals LIS(p). This is correct: In the poset where i j and w_i <= w_j, the width is LIS. Actually, Dilworth's theorem states that the size of a minimum chain decomposition equals the size of a maximum antichain. For a sequence, the poset is defined by i j and w_i <= w_j (i.e., increasing subsequences correspond to chains). Then the minimum number of antichains needed to partition the set equals the size of the longest chain (LIS). But the solution uses decreasing subsequences, which correspond to antichains? Let's recall: In a permutation, an increasing subsequence is a set of indices i1 i2 ... ik such that p(i1) p(i2) ... p(ik). That's a chain in the partial order where (i,p(i)) are points with the product order. A decreasing subsequence is an antichain? Actually, a decreasing subsequence is a set of indices i1 i2 ... ik such that p(i1) > p(i2) > ... > p(ik). This is a chain in the order where i1 i2 and p(i1) > p(i2) (i.e., opposite direction). But the partial order we consider for Dilworth's theorem is the product order: (i1, p(i1)) <= (i2, p(i2)) if i1 <= i2 and p(i1) <= p(i2). Then a chain corresponds to an increasing subsequence, and an antichain corresponds to a decreasing subsequence (since for a decreasing subsequence, you cannot have both coordinates increasing). So 