lumn 1 is indeed a property of a \"horizontal strip\" covering the left region: Since L is defined as the set of cells left of the permutation matrix, each row i has cells from column 1 to p(i)-1. So any rectangle that lies entirely within L can be shifted leftwards until its left side is at column 1, as long as all rows covered have at least that many columns. However, if some row has width less than the rectangle's leftmost column, then we cannot shift leftwards. But the rectangle's leftmost column must be <= min_{i in rows} w_i + 1? Actually, if we have rows a..b, each row i has width w_i = p(i)-1. The rectangle's leftmost column L must satisfy L >= 1 and L <= w_i for each i (since rectangle must be within columns 1..w_i). So the rectangle's leftmost column must be <= min_i w_i. So we can shift leftwards to column 1 only if min_i w_i >= 1 (i.e., all rows have at least one cell). If some row has w_i = 0 (i.e., p(i) = 1), then that row has no left region. Then any rectangle covering that row cannot exist in L. So any rectangle covering rows that have w_i = 0 cannot be in L. So any rectangle in L must be formed by rows with w_i >= 1. For those rows, we can shift leftwards to column 1 because column 1 is within the left region for each such row. So indeed any rectangle in L can be assumed to start at column 1, because we can always shift leftwards until column 1 without leaving L (since column 1 is present in each row of the rectangle). However, if some row has w_i = 0, then that row cannot be part of the rectangle. So the rectangle's rows must be a subset of rows where w_i >= 1. So the rectangle's left side can be column 1. So the claim that any rectangle in L must have left side on column 1 is valid if we consider only rectangles covering rows where w_i >= 1. But is it possible to have a rectangle that starts at column >1 but still within L? Suppose we have rows i where w_i >= 5, and we choose a rectangle covering columns 2..4. Then we could shift leftwards to colu