vering rows R and columns C corresponds to rank-1 matrix 1_R 1_C^T. That's correct.\n\n- They say \"with R∩C=∅, otherwise a diagonal square would be covered\". Actually they require that the rectangle does not contain any uncovered square. The condition is that the rectangle's rows and columns sets must avoid the uncovered squares. They wrote \"R∩C=∅, otherwise a diagonal square would be covered\". This is a bit ambiguous: R∩C = ∅ means the rectangle does not include any squares where row index equals column index? But the uncovered squares are not necessarily on the diagonal; they are at positions (i, sigma(i)). So the condition should be that for all i in R, sigma(i) not in C. The statement \"R∩C=∅\" is insufficient and possibly inaccurate. However they are using the representation of the matrix as 1_R 1_C^T, which includes all squares with row in R and column in C. If any uncovered square (i, sigma(i)) lies in that rectangle, then the tile would cover an uncovered square, violating the condition. So the condition is indeed that the rectangle must avoid the uncovered squares. They wrote \"with R∩C=∅, otherwise a diagonal square would be covered\". This is a simplification assuming sigma is identity (diagonal). However they haven't yet fixed sigma. But later they consider the diagonal case. So this statement might be a minor inaccuracy: they assume diagonal uncovered squares. However they later use the diagonal construction, so it's okay. But the statement may be considered a justification gap: they didn't fully justify that the condition for a tile to avoid uncovered squares is R∩C=∅ in the general case. However it's a minor gap.\n\n- They claim \"Since each summand has rank 1, rank(M) ≤ sum rank = t\". That's valid.\n\n- They claim \"Thus any tiling must satisfy t ≥ rank(M)\". That's valid.\n\n- They claim rank(M) = 2025. They give a proof: show J-P invertible. They argue that for vector 1, (J-P)1 = 2024*1 ≠ 0. For any v orthogonal to 1, Jv = 0, (J-P)v = -Pv, and