l. But the solution's lower bound t >= 2025 is based on rank = 2025. So the rank argument is flawed for some permutations. However, the solution chooses a specific permutation: they later choose the main diagonal (identity permutation). For identity permutation, J - I is invertible, rank = n. So the lower bound holds for that specific permutation? Wait, the lower bound should hold for any tiling that covers all squares except those in H. H is the set of uncovered squares. The rank lower bound uses the matrix M = J - P where P corresponds to H. The rank of M depends on the permutation sigma. If sigma is identity, rank = n. If sigma is something else, rank may be less. The solution wants a lower bound that holds for any possible H (any set of uncovered squares satisfying exactly one per row and column). The rank of J - P may vary with sigma. So the claim that rank(M) = 2025 for any permutation sigma is false. They attempted to prove it by showing that J-P is invertible for any permutation matrix P, but that is not correct. The proof they gave is flawed: they claim that for any vector v orthogonal to all-ones vector, Jv = 0, and (J-P)v = -Pv, and since Pv ≠ 0 for v ≠ 0, (J-P)v ≠ 0. However, they assume that Pv ≠ 0 for any nonzero v orthogonal to all-ones. But that's not true: there exist vectors v orthogonal to all-ones such that Pv = v (i.e., eigenvectors of P with eigenvalue 1) and also orthogonal to all-ones. For example, for the transposition permutation on n=2, vector v = (1,-1) is orthogonal to all-ones (sum zero) and Pv = (-1,1) = -v, not equal to v, but still nonzero. Actually Pv ≠ 0. But the argument that (J-P)v ≠ 0 holds because Pv ≠ 0. However, (J-P)v = -Pv, which is nonzero. So that part is okay. But the determinant argument they gave: \"det(J-P) = (-1)^{2024}(2025-1) = 2024\" is also suspect. Let's compute determinant of J - I for n=2025: eigenvalues: n-1 = 2024 (multiplicity 1) and -1 (multiplicity n-1 = 2024). So determinant = (2024)*(-1)^{2024} = 2024 (