g a column that includes an uncovered square cannot include that square. So rectangle must avoid uncovered squares. So each rectangle must be a product of a set of rows and a set of columns that does not intersect any uncovered square. That means for any row i, the set of columns covered by a tile cannot include sigma(i). Similarly for any column j, the set of rows covered cannot include sigma^{-1}(j). So each tile's row set R and column set C must satisfy that for all i in R, sigma(i) not in C; and for all j in C, sigma^{-1}(j) not in R. That is, the rectangle cannot contain any uncovered square. So the uncovered squares form a \"forbidden\" set: we cannot have any tile that includes any of those positions.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the complement of a permutation matrix with rectangles that avoid the permutation entries. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n (the size of the matrix) for any permutation? Actually this is reminiscent of covering the complement of a permutation matrix with combinatorial rectangles (i.e., rank-1 matrices). The rank argument gave a lower bound of n. And we have a construction achieving n (the row-wise covering). So the lower bound is tight. So the minimal number of tiles is indeed n. So the solution is optimal.\n\nThus Level 1.5: Check optimality. We need to test small cases n=3,4,5. The solution's construction uses n tiles. Could there be a construction with fewer than n tiles? The rank argument says no: rank(M) = n, so t >= n. So the lower bound is proven. So the solution's optimality is correct. However we need to see if the solution's lower bound proof is valid. The rank argument uses that each tile corresponds to a rank-1 matrix, and sum of rank-1 matrices yields M, so rank(M) <= t. This is valid: rank of sum <= sum of ranks, but we need to ensure that the matrices are disjoint (non-overlapping) and that the sum representation is correct. They say \"p