it lies left of the uncovered cell in every row it uses) or *right\u2011leaning* (it lies right of the uncovered cell in every row it uses).  \nThus the left part  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<h_i\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be tiled solely by left\u2011leaning rectangles, and the right part  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>h_i\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be tiled solely by right\u2011leaning rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left part\n\nConsider a column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nBecause \\((h_1,\\dots ,h_n)\\) is a permutation, there exists a unique row \\(r\\) such that \\(h_r=k+1\\).  \nThe cell \\((r,k)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** cell of the left interval in row \\(r\\); the cell \\((r,k+1)\\) is uncovered.\n\nIf a left\u2011leaning rectangle \\(T\\) contains \\((r,k)\\), then its column set \\(C\\) must satisfy \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); otherwise it would also contain column \\(k+1\\) and would cover the uncovered cell \\((r,k+1)\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence column \\(k\\) is the **rightmost column** of the rectangle that covers \\((r,k)\\).\n\nSince a rectangle possesses a unique rightmost column, distinct values \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) require distinct rectangles.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011leaning rectangles are necessary to tile \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right part\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the right side works with the columns to the right of each uncovered cell.  \nFor each \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there is a unique row \\(r\\) with \\(h_r=k-1\\); the cell \\((r,k)\\) is the **leftmost** cell of the right interval in that row, and any right\u2011leaning rectangle covering it cannot contain column \\(k-1\\).  \nThus column \\(k\\) must be the leftmost column of a distinct right\u2011leaning rectangle.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011leaning rectangles are required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Combined lower bound\n\nSumming the two parts, any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place\n\n* a tile \\(T_i^{\\text{left}} = \\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,h_i-1\\}\\) of size \\(1\\times (h_i-1)\\) (if \\(h_i>1\\));\n* a tile \\(T_i^{\\text{right}} = \\{i\\}\\times\\{h_i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of size \\(1\\times (n-h_i)\\) (if \\(h_i<n\\)).\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different rows.  \nEvery cell of \\(L\\) appears in exactly one \\(T_i^{\\text{left}}\\), and every cell of \\(R\\) appears in exactly one \\(T_i^{\\text{right}}\\); the uncovered cells \\((i,h_i)\\) are left empty.  \n\nThe number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{i:h_i>1\\}+\\#\\{i:h_i<n\\}= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide; hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the minimum number of rectangular tiles Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
