  \\]\n\n   These \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, cover every non\u2011diagonal cell, and avoid the diagonal empty cells.  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable, so it is optimal. With \\(n=2025\\) the minimal number of tiles is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares (the *covered* squares) have to be partitioned into axis\u2011parallel rectangles whose interiors are disjoint and whose sides lie on grid lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for any permutation\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the covered squares split into two (possibly empty) horizontal\nsegments:\n\n* the **left segment**  \n  \\[\n  L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n  \\]\n* the **right segment**  \n  \\[\n  R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i ,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint and together contain all covered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (left part).  \nAny tiling of \\(L\\) by rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(T)=\\min\\{j\\mid\\exists\\,i\\;(i,j)\\in T\\}\n\\]\n\nbe its **leftmost column**.  \nBecause \\(T\\subseteq L\\), all its cells lie strictly left of the empty cell in\ntheir rows; consequently \\(\\ell(T)\\le n-1\\).\n\nFor each column index \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) there exists a row\n\\(i\\) such 